# A monad, the structure behind Seed's data types and effects (Option, error handling, sequencing), modeled and its laws
# proved by the kernel. A monad packages a value into a context (return) and chains context-producing steps (bind), and it
# must obey three laws: returning then binding is just applying the step (left identity), binding with return changes
# nothing (right identity), and chaining is associative. The example is the OPTION monad (a value that may be absent), the
# basis of safe error handling. The kernel proves the right-identity law and a left-identity instance by computation over
# the two shapes. (The general laws quantify over an arbitrary continuation; the kernel computes an applied NAMED function
# but leaves an applied unknown one symbolic, so the universal forms await higher-order reduction, while the canonical and
# concrete continuations below are fully checked.) This is the formal heart of how Seed threads data and sequences effects.

form bit
  case off
  case on

task flip
  take b, like bit
  like bit
  fork case, read b
    case off
      send back
        make on
    case on
      send back
        make off

# The Option type: a value present (some) or absent (none).
form opt
  case none
  case some
    link value, like bit

# return / unit: wrap a value in the context.
task ret
  take x, like bit
  like opt
  send back
    make some
      bind value
        read x

# TEMPLATE: the body of a bind over the Option shape. Run a context, propagate absence, and on a present value feed it to
# the continuation task `step`. Replaces the repeated `fork case, read m / case none / make none / case some / send back`
# block shared by bind and its return- and flip-inlined specializations.
tree chain-body
  take step

  hook fuse
    fork case, read m
      case none
        send back
          make none
      case some
        link value
        send back
          call {step}
            read value

# bind: run a context, and if it has a value, feed it to the continuation. The general higher-order operation.
task bind
  take m, like opt
  take f
    like task
      take x, like bit
      like opt
  like opt
  fuse chain-body, f

# bind with RETURN as the continuation, the right-identity instance (return inlined so the kernel computes it).
task chain-return
  take m, like opt
  like opt
  fuse chain-body, ret

# RIGHT IDENTITY: bind m return = m. Binding a context with return leaves it unchanged, for every option. Proved by
# induction over the two shapes (absent stays absent, present rewraps to the same present value).
rule monad-right-identity
  seat m, like opt
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call chain-return
        read m
      read m
  fold m

# A concrete continuation, to witness the left-identity law: wrap the flipped value.
task flip-opt
  take x, like bit
  like opt
  send back
    make some
      bind value
        call flip
          read x

# bind with that concrete continuation inlined.
task chain-flip
  take m, like opt
  like opt
  fuse chain-body, flip-opt

# LEFT IDENTITY (instance): bind (return a) k = k a, here for the continuation k = flip-opt. Wrapping a value with return
# then binding is the same as applying the step directly, for every value a. Holds by computation.
rule monad-left-identity-instance
  seat a, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call chain-flip
        call ret
          read a
      call flip-opt
        read a
  calm hold

# NONE SHORT-CIRCUITS: binding an absent value runs no continuation, the failure propagates. This is why the Option monad
# models safe error handling, a single absence ends the chain.
rule none-short-circuits
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call chain-flip
        make none
      make none
  calm hold
